    "field": "Arctic climate change",
    "problem": "Existing explanations for strong seasonal Arctic warming focus primarily on climate feedbacks, failing to fully account for how the timing of the radiative forcing itself governs the magnitude and pattern of the warming and contributes to large uncertainties among climate models.",
    "method": "The authors use a state-of-the-art global climate model in idealized simulations, applying an artificial longwave radiative forcing to the Arctic for each season separately to quantify the effect of forcing seasonality on the magnitude and pattern of Arctic warming and sea ice decline.",
    "task": "The task is to use a global climate model with idealized seasonal forcing simulations to determine how the timing of radiative forcing governs the magnitude and seasonal pattern of Arctic warming and sea ice decline.",
